WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:12.049 --> 00:00:16.029 align:start position:0%
 
June 13 2014  started as a routine Friday in  Redemption Hospital in Monrovia the  capital of Liberia redemption is the  largest free Public Health Hospital in  the city we are called upon to serve  hundreds of thousands of people in the  best of times that puts a strain on our  resources monthly supplies run out  within weeks and patients of our beds  will be seated in chairs that summer we  had a nurse who had been sick for a  while sick enough to be admitted in our  Hospital but our treatment didn't seem  to be helping her her symptoms were  getting worse diarrhea severe abdominal  pain fever and weakness on that  particular Friday she developed severe  respiratory distress and her eyes were  menacingly red one of my fellow doctors  a general surgeon became suspicious of  her condition he said her symptoms were  suggestive of Ebola we kept a close  watch on her we tried to help her we  were treating her for malaria typhoid  and gastroenteritis we didn't know it  but by then it was too late  the next morning I walk in to check on  my patient I could tell by the look on  her eyes that she was filled with fear I  gave her a reassurance but shortly after  she died of Ebola for me what there was  very personal but this was just the  beginning a virtual biological bomb had  exploded but the war spread faster than  the virus and panic spread across the  hospital all the patients ran away then  all the nurses and doctors ran away this  was the beginning of our medical tsunami  the devastating Ebola virus that left an  indelible scar in our country's history  I was not trained for this I had just  graduated from medical school two years  before at this time my total knowledge  about Ebola came from a wound  article I had read in medical school I  perceived the disease as so dangerous  there's one page in essence she had  convinced me to run out of the hospital  - the moment I heard of a case of Ebola  but when it finally happened I stayed on  and decided to help and so that several  other brave healthcare professionals but  we would pay a heavy price many persons  and health professionals have become  high-risk contacts this actual admit 21  days counting to potentially disease or  death our health systems were fragile  our health workers lack the skills and  treating so you know weeks and months  that followed health workers were  disproportionately affected by the Ebola  virus disease more than 400 nurses  doctors and other health professionals  became infected unfortunately my friend  the general surgeon who correctly  identified the symptoms in that first  case became one of the casualties on  July 27 the President of Liberia imposed  quarantine on the worst affected areas  she closed all the schools and  universities and shut down many public  events four days later the United States  peace cops pulled out of Liberia Sierra  Leone and Guinea due to Ebola in August  six weeks after the nurse died hundreds  of people were dying of disease each  week people were dying in the street  over the months that followed was Africa  will lost thousands of people to Ebola  virus disease in August I joined a team  to set up the Ebola treatment unit at  JFK Hospital in Monrovia I was charged  with running the second Ebola treatment  unit in the city our unit provided hope  for thousands of patients families and  communities I not only provide care I  came face-to-face with Ebola living  everyday asahara's Ebola virus disease  contact during the worst of the outbreak  was one of my worst experiences I  started counting 21 days ever  day I live every moment anticipating the  onset of symptoms of the disease I  measured my body temperature several  times I showered with chlorinated water  more concentrated and actually  recommended a chlorine it at my phones  my pens my hands my car my clothes  became bleached those days you were  alone people were so afraid of touching  anybody  everyone's was counted as a potential  contact touching will make them sick  I was stigmatized but if that was what  it was for me  who was symptom-free imagine what it was  for someone who actually has symptoms  someone who had Ebola we learned that to  treat Ebola successfully we had to  suspend some of the normal rules of  society our president declared a state  of emergency in August and suspended  certain rights and the National Police  even supported our work during the Ebola  response in Feb 2015 came in for  isolation in our bola isolation unit  they were also known as the VIP boys of  Monrovia terrified small-time drug  addicts whose presence could instill  tremendous amount of fear or door they  could not legally carry guns they  underwent quarantine for 21 days in our  unit and was not arrested we told the  police if you arrest them here they will  stop coming they won't get treated and  the boulevards will continue to spread  the police agreed and we were able to  treat the VRP boys and they did not have  to worry about being arrested why in the  unit over the cause of the outbreak West  Africa had almost 29 thousand cases more  than 11,000 people died and that  included twelve of my fine colleagues at  John F Kennedy hospital in Monrovia  in June 2016 exactly 23 months after my  first Ebola patient died  Liberia declared its Ebola outbreak  ended we thought that was the outbreak  ended so did the problems we hope that  life will go back to normal today there  are more than 17,000 survivors in West  Africa people who actually had Ebola  virus disease lived through it and  survived we counted the survival rate as  a success the end of suffering for the  patient and fulfilling joyful families  every discharged from the unit was a  moment of jubilation at least so we  thought the best description of the  moment of discharge and a rare glimpse  into the moment that defines our life  post Ebola was vividly expressed in the  words of my best friend and fellow dr.  Philip Ireland in an interview of the  times he said at the time of his release  there were a lot of people there from  JFK Hospital my family my elder brother  my wife was there a lot of other doctors  were there too and members of the media  were there and I felt like Nelson  Mandela it felt like a long walk to  freedom and I walk and raise my hands to  the heaven thanking God for saving my  life and Phillips said then I saw  something else there were a lot of  crying people people happy to see me but  when I got close to anybody  they backed away for many Ebola  survivors society still seems to be  backing away even as they struggle to  lead a normal life for these survivors  life can be compared to another health  emergency  Demi suffered debilitating joint and  body pain the suffering gradually decays  over time for most however many continue  to bear intermittent pain some survivors  are blind others have neurological  disabilities some survivors experience  stigmatization every day in many ways a  lot of children high offense some  survivors experience post-traumatic  stress disorder and some survivors like  opportunity for education  even families can be split apart by fear  of Ebola - there's no definitive cue for  transmitting Ebola virus to sex however  there are successful interventions for  prevention we have worked hard on semen  testing behavioral counseling safe sex  promotion and research for the past year  there have been no cases of sexual  transmission but some mill survivors  have lost their spouses out of fear will  be infected with Ebola that's how  families are torn apart another  tremendous challenge for Ebola survivors  is obtaining adequate health care in  theory  Liberia's public health services are  free of charge in practice our health  system locked the funding and capacity  to expand care to all at the point of  need many survivors have waited many  most undergo surgery to heal they're  blinding cataracts whew how to relive  the traumatic experience when her blood  was retested for a bullet at a point of  admission some survivors experience  delayed a deferred admission due to  limited bear capacity no better vailable  for one more patient this is neither  national policy no official con dude but  many people are still afraid of the  sporadic resurgence of Ebola virus the  results can be tragic I have seen  Beatrice in Ebola survivor clinic  several times now she's 26 years old  many of her family members became  infected  she luckily survived but since that day  in 2014 she was discharged - caring  health workers her life has never been  the same she became blind as a result of  Ebola in 2014 the baby of a dear friend  of mine was only two months old when  both parents and child were admitted in  an Ebola treatment unit in Monrovia  luckily they survived my friend's baby  is almost three years old now but cannot  stand cannot walk he cannot speak he has  failure to thrive there are many more  hidden experiences and many stories are  yet untold  the survivors of Ebola deserves our  attention and support the only way we  can defeat this pandemic is when we  ensure that we win this final battle our  best opportunities to ensure that every  survivor we sees adequate care at the  point of need without any form of stigma  and had no cost to them personally  how can a society consider itself healed  when a person's entire identity is  defined by the fact that they recovered  from Ebola she were previous disease  that a person no longer has become the  sum total of the identity the  identifiers in the passport that it  turns you from traveling to seek medical  care abroad simply the ID that denies  you health care or prevents you from  having a relationship with your spouse  or denies your family or friend or whom  or prevents you from carrying on your  normal job so you can put food on the  table or have a roof over your family's  head what is the meaning of the right to  life when our life is clouded by stigma  and barriers that filled our stigma  until we have much better answers to  those questions in West Africa our work  is not over yet Liberians  are resilient people and we know how to  rise to a challenge even a devastating  one my best memories of the outbreak  centered on those many people who  survived the disease but I cannot forget  the hard-working nurses doctors  volunteers and staff who risked their  own safety in service of humanity and  some even losing their lives in the  process  during the worst of the contagion one  thing cap was making those perilous  delayed journeys into the boulevards we  had a passion to save life but I afraid  during a bola outbreak of course I was  but for me the opportunity to protect  our global health security and keep  communities safe at home and  Brod was an honor so as the dangers  became greater our humanity became  stronger we faced our fears the global  health community working together  defeated Ebola and that that is how I  know that we can defeat Earth's  aftermath in our hearts in our minds and  in our communities thank you  [Applause]  [Music]

